:: Loc_home
The narrow hallway of 14 Elm Road: coats on hooks, a pile of shoes nobody claims, and the post on the mat. The living room is through the door ahead, with the kitchen off to the side; your bedroom and the bathroom are past the living room.
<<if setup.text.period() is "night">>The house is dark apart from the streetlight through the glass in the front door.<</if>>

:: Loc_home_bedroom
Your room. A single bed under the window, a desk buried in revision guides you keep meaning to open, and a wardrobe that doesn't quite shut.
<<switch setup.text.period()>>
<<case "morning">>Grey morning light leaks round the edges of the curtains.
<<case "afternoon">>The afternoon sun lights up the dust over your desk.
<<case "evening">>The desk lamp throws a warm circle over the clutter.
<<default>>It's dark out. The glow of your phone charger is the only light.
<</switch>>

:: Loc_home_bathroom
A small bathroom with a shower over the bath, a mirror that steams up instantly, and a cabinet full of half-used bottles.

:: Loc_home_kitchen
The kitchen smells faintly of toast. There's a fridge covered in takeaway menus, a kettle that's always warm, and enough in the cupboards to make something decent if you can be bothered.

:: Loc_home_living_room
A squashy sofa, a TV that's slightly too big for the room, and a coffee table covered in remote controls. It's the comfiest room in the house.

:: Loc_elm_road
Elm Road is a long terrace of red-brick houses with small front gardens. The Route 7 bus stops halfway down, and taxis can usually be flagged here.
<<switch setup.text.period()>>
<<case "morning">>People hurry past to the bus stop, coffee cups in hand.
<<case "afternoon">>It's quiet: a few parked cars and a cat asleep on a wall.
<<case "evening">>Windows glow orange up and down the street, and somebody's cooking something with garlic.
<<default>>The street is silent under the orange streetlights.
<</switch>>

:: Loc_corner_shop
Patel's Corner Shop is crammed floor to ceiling: newspapers, sweets, a fridge of cold drinks, and a little bit of everything else. The bell over the door jingles when you come in.

:: Loc_elm_park_green
A patch of open grass at the end of Elm Road, with a few old oak trees, a play area and a path down to the duck pond. Dog walkers nod at each other as they pass.

:: Loc_elm_green_pond
A small, slightly murky pond with a bench beside it. The ducks know exactly what a rustling bag means.

:: Loc_academy
Mooresville Academy: a big brick building from the sixties with a newer glass wing bolted on. Students mill round the front entrance before the bell. After school, the library, the sports hall and the playing field stay open until five.
<<if setup.time.weekday(setup.time.now()) is 0 or setup.time.weekday(setup.time.now()) is 6>>It's the weekend, and the place is locked up.<</if>>

:: Loc_academy_form_room
Room 12B is your form room: rows of desks, a whiteboard that never quite wipes clean, and a poster about exam stress that someone has drawn a moustache on.

:: Loc_academy_canteen
The canteen is loud and smells of chips. Long tables, plastic chairs, and a queue that moves just slowly enough to be annoying. A vending machine hums in the corner.

:: Loc_academy_library
The library is the quietest place in school. Tall shelves, study carrels by the windows, and Ms Rowe's desk positioned to see everything.

:: Loc_academy_sports_hall
A big echoing hall with lines painted all over the floor and basketball hoops at each end. It smells of rubber and floor polish.

:: Loc_academy_field
The playing field stretches out behind the school, muddy round the goalmouths. On a clear day you can see over the rooftops to the river.

:: Loc_academy_boys_toilets
The boys' toilets: cold, echoey and covered in graffiti about people who left years ago.

:: Loc_academy_girls_toilets
The girls' toilets: a queue for the one working mirror, and graffiti about people who left years ago.

:: Loc_high_street
Mooresville High Street is the heart of town: a mix of old shopfronts and chain stores, with stops for the Route 7 and Route 3 buses and a taxi rank by the old post office. The Daily Grind, The Red Lion and Threadline are all close by.
<<switch setup.text.period()>>
<<case "morning">>Delivery vans are unloading and shops are pulling up their shutters.
<<case "afternoon">>The pavements are busy with shoppers and people on their lunch breaks.
<<case "evening">>The shops are shutting, and the pub is getting busier.
<<default>>Most of the shops are dark. Taxis idle at the rank.
<</switch>>

:: Loc_daily_grind
The Daily Grind is a narrow café with mismatched chairs, plants in every window and a chalkboard menu. The coffee machine hisses constantly.

:: Loc_grind_counter
A wooden counter stacked with pastries under glass domes. The barista works the coffee machine with practised speed.

:: Loc_grind_seating
Small tables and a long bench by the window. It's a good place to sit with a drink and watch the high street go by.

:: Loc_grind_toilet
A single small toilet with a fern on the cistern and a sign asking you, very politely, not to flush anything else.

:: Loc_red_lion
The Red Lion is an old coaching inn with low beams and a crooked front. Inside it's warm and dim, with a long bar, a lounge with a pool table, and a beer garden out the back.

:: Loc_lion_bar
The bar is lined with pumps and polished brass. A chalkboard lists the guest ales and the pub quiz scores. Gary watches the room from behind the till.

:: Loc_lion_lounge
The lounge has worn leather seats, a fireplace and a pool table with a slight lean that the regulars know how to use.

:: Loc_lion_beer_garden
A walled garden with picnic benches, fairy lights strung overhead and a couple of patio heaters.

:: Loc_lion_toilets
The pub toilets: down a narrow corridor past the kitchen, with a hand dryer that roars like a jet engine.

:: Loc_wellbeing
The Mooresville Wellbeing Centre's waiting room: pale wood, soft lighting, comfy chairs and a rack of leaflets on everything from sleep to money worries. Tom sits at the curved reception desk by the door, next to a bowl of mints and a phone that never stops ringing. The therapy room is through the door at the back.

:: Loc_wellbeing_therapy
A small, warm room with two armchairs, a box of tissues on the table and a window that looks onto a tree. Dr Okafor's notebook sits closed on her knee.

:: Loc_wellbeing_toilets
Clean, quiet toilets that smell of lavender hand soap.

:: Loc_threadline
Threadline is a small clothing boutique with exposed brick walls, rails of carefully chosen clothes and a playlist that's just a bit too cool.

:: Loc_threadline_floor
Rails of jackets, jeans and knitwear, arranged by colour. A table at the front displays this week's new arrivals.

:: Loc_threadline_fitting
Two fitting rooms behind velvet curtains, each with a flattering mirror and a hook that's always slightly too small.

:: Loc_pulsefit
PulseFit is a busy gym above a row of shops, all mirrors, motivational slogans and thumping music.

:: Loc_pulsefit_reception
A bright front desk with a membership screen and a basket of free protein-bar samples.

:: Loc_pulsefit_floor
Rows of treadmills facing the windows, racks of weights, and the clang of someone dropping a barbell.

:: Loc_pulsefit_changing
Changing rooms with lockers, benches and a row of showers with excellent water pressure.

:: Loc_arcade
The Arcade Shopping Centre is a big glass-roofed mall. The main concourse is bright and busy, with escalators, a fountain and benches where people rest their shopping bags, and every shop opens off it.

:: Loc_arcade_food_court
A ring of fast-food counters round a sea of plastic tables. It's noisy, it smells of everything at once, and it's always busy.

:: Loc_arcade_freshway
FreshWay is a big supermarket with bright aisles, a meal-deal fridge by the door and self-checkouts that always need an assistant.

:: Loc_arcade_urban_thread
Urban Thread is a big high-street clothing chain, with loud music, endless rails and a sale section at the back.

:: Loc_urban_thread_fitting
A long row of fitting cubicles. A staff member counts your items with a tired smile.

:: Loc_arcade_cinema
The Arcade Cinema has six screens, a popcorn counter and posters for this week's films. It stays open after the rest of the centre shuts.

:: Loc_arcade_toilets
Busy shopping-centre toilets with a long line of sinks and a cleaner who's seen it all.

:: Loc_riverside_park
Riverside Park follows the River Moore through the middle of town, with wide lawns, old willows, a bandstand nobody plays in any more and a boathouse kiosk near the bridge. The Route 3 bus stops at the gates.
<<switch setup.text.period()>>
<<case "morning">>Joggers and dog walkers have the place to themselves, and the grass is still wet with dew.
<<case "afternoon">>People are dotted across the lawns: picnics, kickabouts, someone asleep under a newspaper.
<<case "evening">>The light goes golden over the water, and couples drift along the paths.
<<default>>The lamps along the paths make small islands of light, with a lot of dark in between.
<</switch>>

:: Loc_riverside_toilets
Concrete park toilets with a sign about opening hours and a hand dryer that gives up halfway.

:: Loc_riverside_path
A gravel path runs along the water's edge, past rowing boats and the occasional heron. There are benches every few hundred metres.

:: Loc_riverside_kiosk
A little green kiosk by the old boathouse selling ice creams, teas and snacks through a hatch.

:: Loc_snips
Snips is a bright little salon with three chairs, a wall of mirrors and a stack of style magazines from last year. It smells of shampoo and hairspray, and the radio is always on.

:: Loc_riverside_court
A tidy brick block of flats overlooking the river. The shared hallway smells faintly of other people's cooking, and the post is stacked on a shelf by the door.

:: Loc_flat_living
<<when { livesAt: "riverside_flat" }>>Your own place. A small living room with a window onto the river, and doors to the bedroom, bathroom and kitchen.<</when>>

:: Loc_flat_bedroom
A box room with just enough space for a bed and a rail of clothes. It's yours, though.

:: Loc_flat_bathroom
A narrow bathroom with a shower over the bath and an extractor fan that sounds like a jet engine.

:: Loc_flat_kitchen
A galley kitchen: two hobs, a microwave and a fridge that hums all night.

:: Loc_keys_lettings
A glass-fronted office on the corner of Sovereign Square, its window full of photos of flats and houses with prices that make you wince. Someone behind the desk looks up and smiles.

:: Loc_community_centre
A low brick building near the Academy, its noticeboard covered in flyers for clubs, classes and lost cats.

:: Loc_community_hall
A big echoey hall with a stage at one end and stacks of plastic chairs along the walls.

:: Loc_community_toilets
Clean enough, with a hand dryer that roars.

:: Loc_calm_waters
Behind a quiet door between two shops on the High Street, with a brass plaque reading Calm Waters Hypnotherapy, is a small waiting room: soft chairs, a water cooler and a pile of old magazines. The therapy room is through the door at the back.

:: Loc_calm_waters_room
A dim, calm room with a deep reclining chair, heavy curtains and a clock that ticks softly.

:: Loc_business_district
Sovereign Square is all glass towers and clipped hedges, with a fountain in the middle and a Route 3 stop on the corner. Keys & Co Estate Agents, Northern Crown Bank and the long, low hangars of Grumman Productions all open off it, and there's a cash machine in the wall that works day and night.
<<switch setup.text.period()>>
<<case "morning">>Suits and lanyards pour across the square, coffee cups in hand, nobody looking up.
<<case "afternoon">>Office workers eat lunch on the edge of the fountain, scrolling their phones.
<<case "evening">>The towers empty out. A few lit windows, a cleaner pushing a trolley, the hiss of the fountain.
<<default>>The square is deserted. The fountain has been switched off, and the cash machine glows in the dark.
<</switch>>

:: Loc_crown_bank
Northern Crown Bank: marble floors, a row of glass-fronted counters and a queue that moves one polite step at a time. Pens on little chains, leaflets about mortgages, and the hush of a place that deals with money.

:: Loc_grumman_studios
The reception of Grumman Productions is louder than you'd expect: posters for films you half remember, a framed award nobody dusts, and crew in black T-shirts hurrying through with cables and coffee. A desk, a sofa for visitors, and a sign on the far door: QUIET WHEN THE RED LIGHT IS ON.

:: Loc_grumman_casting
A cramped office with a camera on a tripod, a single chair in front of a white wall, and a cork board covered in headshots. A list on the door reads "Extras: leave your details".

:: Loc_grumman_stage
A huge, dark hangar. Most of it is empty concrete; in one corner, a set is lit as bright as day: a living room with three walls and no ceiling. Cables snake everywhere.

:: Loc_grumman_dressing
A corridor of small dressing rooms: mirrors ringed with bulbs, clothes rails, and a shower that takes a full minute to run hot.

:: Loc_south_town
South Town is the old docks: brick warehouses turned into bars, a few shuttered shops, and a Route 3 stop by the harbour wall. The purple sign of Club Neon hangs over the street, Scarlet's has its windows tastefully frosted, and the Golden Grill's light never seems to go off.
<<switch setup.text.period()>>
<<case "morning">>It's quiet: shutters down, a street sweeper, gulls going through the bins.
<<case "afternoon">>A few people drift between the bars and the bookies. The harbour smells of diesel and salt.
<<case "evening">>The neon signs flicker on one by one, and music starts leaking out of doorways.
<<default>>The street belongs to the night: taxis, shouting, laughter, and the Golden Grill's queue.
<</switch>>

:: Loc_club_neon
A heavy black door under a buzzing purple sign. Inside, the cloakroom hatch and a little desk where you pay and get your hand stamped. The bass from the dance floor makes the walls hum.
<<when { status: "neon_stamp" }>>The stamp on your hand glows under the black light.<</when>>

:: Loc_neon_dance_floor
A dark room packed with bodies, lights sweeping overhead and the bass coming up through the floor. The DJ booth glows at the far end.

:: Loc_neon_bar
A long bar lit from underneath in violet, three deep with people waving cards. The bar staff move fast and hear almost nothing.

:: Loc_neon_vip
A raised booth behind a velvet rope: leather seats, a table of bottles, and a view down over the dance floor.

:: Loc_neon_smoking
A fenced-in yard out the back, strung with fairy lights. People shout conversations over the muffled music and their own breath steams in the air.

:: Loc_neon_toilets
Mirrors crowded with people fixing their make-up, a queue that's half gossip, and a floor you'd rather not look at.

:: Loc_scarlets
Scarlet's is warmer and brighter inside than its frosted windows suggest: rails of lingerie and silky nightwear at the front, soft music, and a bead curtain to the back room. The woman at the counter gives you a friendly, completely unbothered nod.

:: Loc_scarlets_fitting
A small cubicle with a velvet curtain, a hook and a very flattering mirror.

:: Loc_golden_grill
A bright, narrow takeaway with a spinning doner in the window, a fridge of cans, and a counter that's been wiped a thousand times. The menu board is mostly photos.
<<if setup.text.period() is "night">>Half of Club Neon seems to be in here, eating chips with their fingers.<</if>>

:: Loc_beach
The promenade runs along the top of the sea wall: railings, benches, a Route 3 stop and a row of beach huts painted in faded candy colours. Steps lead down to the sand. The changing area is by the steps, the Surf Shack is next to it, and the old pier stretches out over the water.
<<switch setup.text.period()>>
<<case "morning">>Dog walkers and a few hardy swimmers have the beach to themselves.
<<case "afternoon">>The smell of chips and suncream drifts along the promenade.
<<case "evening">>The sun goes down over the water, and the pier lights come on one by one.
<<default>>The sea is a black hush beyond the railings, and the pier is dark.
<</switch>>

:: Loc_beach_sand
A long sweep of pale sand between the sea wall and the waterline, dotted with windbreaks, sandcastles and a lifeguard's chair.

:: Loc_beach_sea
The sea: cold, grey-green and always moving. The waves break in long white lines, and the shore slopes gently enough to wade out.

:: Loc_beach_changing
A row of wooden cubicles with half-doors, a cold outdoor shower for rinsing off the sand, and a couple of toilets. Everything smells of wet towels.

:: Loc_beach_pier
The old pier creaks underfoot. Halfway along there's an amusement arcade full of flashing slot machines; at the end, a fish and chip kiosk and benches looking out to sea.

:: Loc_surf_shack
A wooden hut with bodyboards hanging from the roof, racks of swimwear and flip-flops, and a freezer of ice lollies by the door.
